In this lesson, we'll look at Newton's method, a clever way to track down the solution of an equation using nothing more than a tangent line. Picture yourself guessing where a root might be. You draw the tangent to the curve at that guess, then follow that tangent down to where it crosses the horizontal axis. That crossing point becomes your next, better guess. You just repeat the process, and each step usually pulls you closer to the true root. Now, it works beautifully when the curve is smooth, when your first guess is reasonably close, and crucially when the slope of the tangent is never zero — otherwise you'd be dividing by nothing and the method blows up. But it can also misbehave. If your starting guess lands in the wrong neighbourhood, Newton might happily march off toward a different root entirely. And near points where the curve flattens or bends sharply, the guesses can bounce around or escape to nowhere. Compared with the bisection method, which is slow but safe, Newton is lightning fast once it gets going, but only if the conditions are kind. So picking the right tool really depends on the problem in front of you. Now let's see it in action.